<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · city</p><h1 class="enc-title">La Goulette</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">La Goulette is a port town on the narrow channel linking the Lake of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tunis">Tunis</a> with the Gulf of Tunis, serving as the harbour of Tunis. Its fortress was captured by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/charles-v-holy-roman-emperor">Charles V</a> in 1535 and held by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/spain">Spain</a> until an Ottoman force under Sinan Pasha took it in 1574. In the 19th and 20th centuries it had a large Italian, Maltese and Jewish population.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/la-goulette.jpg" alt="La Goulette" width="320" height="133" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Bengt Nyman · CC BY 2.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Panorama,_La_Goulette.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Spain took Bejaia and Tripoli in 1510, while the brothers Oruç and Hayreddin Barbarossa operated from <b>La Goulette</b> and Jerba with Hafsid permission before founding their own state at Algiers.</blockquote><a href="/empire/hafsid-sultanate">Hafsid Sultanate · Decline &amp; Fall</a></li><li><blockquote>In 1574 an Ottoman expedition under Sinan Pasha took Tunis and <b>La Goulette</b> from Spain and its Hafsid client, ending the Hafsid Sultanate.</blockquote><a href="/state/ottoman-tunisia">Ottoman Tunisia</a></li></ul></section>
